  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 255 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Continued dry with mostly sunny conditions expected \nlate week into the weekend. \n\nSubtropical Storm Josephine continues to pull away from the CONUS \nthis afternoon as high pressure centered over the Great Lakes builds \neastward. Clear skies this afternoon will continue tonight. Low \ntemps are forecast to be a few degrees above what we saw this \nmorning due to slightly stronger winds behind a dry front. Lows will \nmainly be in the low 40s inland upper 40s to low 50s near the water. \nA few of the typically cooler spots could drop into the upper 30s \nprior to sunrise, especially if winds are able to completely \ndecouple for a few hours. Breezy and slightly cooler Friday behind \nthe front with temps ranging from the mid to upper 60s to around 70 \ndegrees. High pressure builds over the region Friday night into \nSaturday. Clear skies and light/calm winds will allow temps to fall \nback into the 30s, mainly for northern/northwestern portions of the \narea. Latest statistical guidance has edged upward regarding low \ntemps Friday night but still could see some frost in the cooler \nspots by sunrise Saturday. \n\nHigh pressure will dominate the local weather pattern this weekend \nand likely well into next week. Not much change in the forecast with \nseasonal temperatures and near zero indications for much needed \nrainfall returning to the region. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Elevated tidal anomalies continue through Friday \nwith minor tidal flooding forecast.\n\nSubtropical Storm Josephine is moving NE away from the coast this \nafternoon. However, large swell continues to propagate toward the \ncoast. Meanwhile, high pressure over ON/QB is gradually building S \nin the wake of Josephine. Tidal anomalies remain ~2ft above normal \nprimarily due to the swell from Josephine. These anomalies should \nremain steady and/or slowly fall tonight into Friday. Therefore, \nprimarily nuisance to minor tidal flooding is expected and any \nlingering Coastal Flood Warnings have been converted to Coastal \nFlood Advisories. Nuisance to locally minor tidal flooding could \nlinger into the weekend as swell continues to propagate toward the \ncoast with persistent (but weaker) onshore flow, as well as a gradual \nincrease in astronomical high tides as we approach full moon.\n\nHigh Surf Advisories continue through 7 PM for VA Beach and Outer \nBanks Currituck.",
